//
// The two things `par` refuses, in the words a reader will see.
//
// The Parallel execution chapter warns about both, and a warning about a refusal is a
// promise about a STRING that no chapter can keep — an `assert` cannot reach a program that
// does not compile.  So the chapter stays readable and the sentences are pinned here.
//
//   * THE METHOD FORM OF A FREE FUNCTION.  A free function is reachable only as `f(a)`;
//     written `a.f()` it is an unknown method, inside `par` as everywhere else.  (A `self`
//     function answers both forms — C123 — and inside `par` it now does too, loft#1803;
//     this file used to pin that as a refusal.)
//   * WHAT MAY BE CAPTURED.  A worker runs on its own store clone, so only the LOOP ELEMENT
//     may be a reference; any other captured argument must be a scalar.  The chapter's
//     "extra arguments from the calling scope" reads as unrestricted, and forwarding a
//     config struct — the obvious use — is refused.
//
// Companion to `the-reference-par-guarantees-hold-for-every-source.loft`, which holds the
// behavioural half.  They are apart on purpose: `loft --tests <file>` SKIPS every running
// cell in a file that carries an `@EXPECT_ERROR`, so a mixed file's running half can only be
// checked through the Rust suite — a broken assertion in one reported `ok` from the CLI.
//
// @falsified-at: none — a LOCK, and it cannot be otherwise: every cell refuses on every
//   build, so there is no control for `make falsify` to move against.
//
//   Measured by hand, ONE CELL AT A TIME in its own file, because a multi-cell
//   `@EXPECT_ERROR` file credits every declaration after a firing one without matching
//   (loft#1261) — run whole, this file cannot tell a live cell from a dead one.  Each of the
//   substrings was replaced in turn by a word the compiler never prints and each cell,
//   alone, failed; restoring it returns each to green.  The running cell at the end is the
//   other direction: it is the shape the third refusal names as the cure, and it works.

struct PS { value: integer }
struct PCfg { factor: integer }

fn p_thrice(p_r: const PS) -> integer { p_r.value * 3 }
fn p_scaled(p_r: const PS, p_c: const PCfg) -> integer { p_r.value * p_c.factor }
fn p_scale(p_r: const PS, p_f: integer) -> integer { p_r.value * p_f }

// A free function is not reachable by the method spelling.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: Unknown method 'p_thrice' on type 'PS'
fn the_method_form_cannot_reach_a_free_function() {
  p_v: vector<PS> = [ PS { value: 4 } ];
  for p_a in p_v par(p_b = p_a.p_thrice(), 2) { assert(p_b == 12, "unreachable"); }
}

// Only the loop element may be a reference; the message says so and names the cure.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: captured argument 'p_c' is a reference
fn a_captured_reference_argument_is_refused() {
  p_v: vector<PS> = [ PS { value: 4 } ];
  p_cfg = PCfg { factor: 3 };
  for p_a in p_v par(p_b = p_scaled(p_a, p_cfg), 2) { assert(p_b == 12, "unreachable"); }
}

// The cure that refusal names, so the file is not passing on its refusals alone.
fn a_forwarded_scalar_is_the_supported_shape() {
  p_v: vector<PS> = [ PS { value: 4 }, PS { value: 5 } ];
  p_factor = 3;
  p_t = 0;
  for p_a in p_v par(p_b = p_scale(p_a, p_factor), 2) { p_t += p_b; }
  assert(p_t == 27, "forwarded scalar: {p_t}");
}
